kernelbase: Don't dereference start before the bounds check in CharPrevExA.

CharPrevExA() evaluates *start before it has established that start is below
ptr, so a call with start == ptr crashes instead of returning start.  The
NULL/NULL case is the one that shows up in the wild: MapleStory TW calls
CharPrevExA(950, NULL, NULL, 0) while building its login screen and takes an
access violation inside kernelbase, which its own crash handler swallows --
the game then exits silently a few seconds later with a black window and no
Wine-side diagnostic at all.

Windows returns ptr for this call.  Put the bounds check back in front so the
dereference can only happen once start is known to be a valid, in-range
pointer.

The wrong order is not new: wine-10.0 has it too, and the game crashes there in
exactly the same way (verified by building wine-10.0 and watching Nexon's own
crash reporter record c0000005 thirteen seconds in).  CrossOver carries the
same one-line fix in its tree, which is why the game never crashed there.


diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/string.c b/dlls/kernelbase/string.c
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/string.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/string.c
@@ -1138,7 +1138,7 @@
 
 LPSTR WINAPI CharPrevExA(WORD codepage, const char *start, const char *ptr, DWORD flags)
 {
-    while (*start && (start < ptr))
+    while ((start < ptr) && *start)
     {
         LPCSTR next = CharNextExA(codepage, start, flags);
         if (next >= ptr) break;
